reward — the scalar score the RM assigns to each generated response. This is the direct signal from RM_buggy or RM_clean. You want this to increase over training, meaning the policy is generating responses the RM prefers. If it plateaus early, either the policy has maximized what the RM can reward, or the RM signal is too easy to saturate (which you've already seen with 100% RM accuracy).
kl — KL divergence between the current policy and the frozen reference policy. Measures how far the policy has drifted from the base model. If this grows too large, the policy is moving far from coherent language generation and likely reward hacking. The adaptive kl_coef tries to keep this near target_kl=6.0. Watch this closely — runaway KL is the most common PPO failure mode.
pg_loss — policy gradient loss, the core RL objective. Measures whether the policy is moving in the direction of higher-reward responses. Should generally decrease (become more negative) as training progresses, meaning the policy is improving at generating responses the RM scores highly.
vf_loss — value function loss, how well the ValueHead is predicting expected rewards. Should decrease as the value function learns to accurately estimate reward. High vf_loss means your advantage estimates are noisy, which makes policy gradient updates less stable. If vf_loss stays high throughout training, the value function isn't learning and you may need a higher vf_coef or a separate optimizer for the value head.

